<section class="welcome guide-welcome"><div class="eyebrow">ACCESS HARBOR</div><h1>Ask. Approve. Expire.</h1><p>Temporary access through your existing identity,<br>with an administrator’s approval and an expiration date.</p></section>
<div class="guide-steps"><article class="panel"><span class="step-number">1</span><h2>Request access</h2><p>Pick GitHub or AWS, a resource, and permission level. Explain the task and choose 1–30 days.</p></article><article class="panel"><span class="step-number">2</span><h2>Get approval</h2><p>Every administrator receives an email. A different person approves or denies the request.</p></article><article class="panel"><span class="step-number">3</span><h2>Access expires</h2><p>The worker grants access and removes it after the duration ends. Provider confirmations are recorded in your request history.</p></article></div>
<div class="note"><strong>No tokens or SSH keys are issued to users.</strong><p>You use your own GitHub identity and AWS SSO. Server credentials come from Secrets Manager. GitHub uses an App private key; AWS uses workload identity.</p></div>
<section id="operators" class="operator-guide"><h2>Set up your environment</h2><ol class="setup-steps"><li><h3>Choose infrastructure</h3><p>For local development, start Floci. It provides the AWS API endpoints for Secrets Manager, Cognito, IAM Identity Center and SES. Production uses AWS and your organization’s OIDC provider.</p><pre><code>docker compose -f compose.floci.yaml up -d
npm run setup</code></pre></li><li><h3>Connect identities and resources</h3><p>Local setup asks you to create your administrator and requester accounts. Production setup enrolls existing OIDC subjects and maps them to GitHub usernames and Identity Center user IDs. There are no built-in accounts.</p></li><li><h3>Store credentials in Secrets Manager</h3><p>Setup writes session and OIDC secrets directly to the manager. GitHub App keys and optional SMTP credentials belong in the same secret bundle. Local files contain metadata and secret references only.</p></li><li><h3>Start the application and worker</h3><pre><code>npm start
# In another terminal:
npm run worker</code></pre><p>Verify sign-in, approval, actual provider access, email delivery and early removal before inviting your team.</p></li></ol></section>
<details class="panel operator-details"><summary>What should I know about expiry?</summary><p>Revocation is best-effort during provider outages, throttling or worker downtime. Removing an AWS assignment does not instantly invalidate existing role sessions. Existing team/group grants may independently provide access. This release manages new direct grants and preserves preexisting access.</p></details>
